00:00:54.893 --> 00:01:01.287
First, we'll go over some projects using Node.js and TypeScript

00:01:01.288 --> 00:01:03.591
and look at what problems existed

00:01:03.592 --> 00:01:07.490
and how Deno was used to solve those problems

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:11.645
Next, I will share improved developer experience using Deno

00:01:11.646 --> 00:01:15.677
In fact, I wanted to write that it was an incomparable development experience

00:01:15.678 --> 00:01:20.214
That is how big of a merit Deno has compared to Node.js

00:01:20.672 --> 00:01:23.925
Today I will talk about Deno

00:01:23.926 --> 00:01:26.611
but Node.js has huge ecology

00:01:26.612 --> 00:01:29.738
There are many legacies being used in Node.js

00:01:29.739 --> 00:01:32.358
so you have be proficient in it as well

00:01:32.359 --> 00:01:34.508
So I will talk about

00:01:34.509 --> 00:01:39.331
how while using Deno you can still work compatibly with Node.js

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:45.708
Lastly, I will share our team's experience on how we used and adapted to using Deno

00:01:48.593 --> 00:01:53.541
Let's consider a situation in which we are making a Node.js project with a TypeScript

00:01:53.986 --> 00:01:59.645
First, we will need a package manager to use the diverse library in the JavaScript ecosystem

00:01:59.646 --> 00:02:04.023
there is npm, yarn, yarn berry and pnpm as well

00:02:04.024 --> 00:02:07.022
We have to choose one package manager

00:02:07.295 --> 00:02:11.077
Next, in order to adjust to code quality and style

00:02:11.078 --> 00:02:15.626
we have to choose some tools like ESLint or Prettier

00:02:15.627 --> 00:02:19.564
And if there are wanted plug-ins, or rules

00:02:19.565 --> 00:02:22.616
we have to add those to the setting

00:02:23.698 --> 00:02:29.077
Then to increase the coding quality we have to add a testing framework

00:02:29.078 --> 00:02:32.159
Jest, Mocha or the recently released ones like Vitest

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.750
these testing frameworks are put in the settings for the project

00:02:37.853 --> 00:02:39.573
But there is more

00:02:39.839 --> 00:02:43.484
Since the TypeScript code can't be used in Node.js right away

00:02:43.485 --> 00:02:45.578
there needs to be transpiler settings

00:02:45.948 --> 00:02:50.017
Using the TypeScript compiler,  after transforming JavaScript code

00:02:50.018 --> 00:02:52.893
and delivered to Node.js

00:02:52.894 --> 00:02:59.665
or transpilers like ts-node, sucrase and swc can be used like a TypeScript compiler

00:02:59.666 --> 00:03:04.503
or through the register hook when the Node.js is loading the TypeScript module

00:03:04.504 --> 00:03:08.609
this can be transferred to a JavaScript transpiler in advance

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:12.269
so that the TypeScript can be used in Node.js

00:03:15.634 --> 00:03:20.364
There are many things to be considered for a simple Node.js project

00:03:20.365 --> 00:03:24.603
Couldn't there be a much faster and simpler way of starting a project?

00:03:24.912 --> 00:03:30.502
It would be great to do modernity coding without all the tasks before

00:03:32.695 --> 00:03:39.197
So I want to introduce to you Deno, a new JavaScript runtime that will solve such problems

00:03:41.277 --> 00:03:45.236
The problems mentioned before can be solved easily with Deno

00:03:45.237 --> 00:03:50.628
First, Deno doesn't use a package manager so there is no need to choose a package manager

00:03:50.629 --> 00:03:53.656
third-party modules are imported through URL

00:03:54.208 --> 00:03:57.016
and packages are saved in the local cache

00:03:58.049 --> 00:04:01.412
Even formatters/linters and tests can be used

00:04:01.413 --> 00:04:04.625
without any installation on Deno through subcommands

00:04:05.331 --> 00:04:08.836
TypeScript can be used without any special settings

00:04:11.887 --> 00:04:18.211
Putting all its strengths aside I want to talk about the improved developer experience of Deno

00:04:18.212 --> 00:04:24.680
I want to highlight a few of the big points I feel are important from the perspective of a developer experience

00:04:24.681 --> 00:04:26.841
I'll talk one by one

00:04:29.518 --> 00:04:36.009
First, Deno has a rich and well-selected standard library that can be used in a variety of situations

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:42.292
Representatively, there are modules like async, bytes, io, streams, http

00:04:42.293 --> 00:04:43.945
I'll deal with one at a time

00:04:43.946 --> 00:04:49.383
async has many utility functions that can be used with Promise

00:04:49.384 --> 00:04:52.058
like debounce, defferred, and delay,

00:04:52.059 --> 00:04:55.903
you don't need to use asynchronous functions  in projects

00:04:55.904 --> 00:04:59.911
through npm installations or make it by itself

00:05:00.604 --> 00:05:05.135
bytes supplies functions that can handle things like Unit8Array

00:05:05.918 --> 00:05:10.765
io provides Buffer that can be used for Deno's reader and write interface

00:05:11.052 --> 00:05:14.349
Now when you want to read and use

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:18.689
Unit8Array or some data

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:22.344
if you follow with Deno's reader and interface

00:05:22.345 --> 00:05:26.395
io library and modules can be of great help

00:05:27.103 --> 00:05:31.753
streams provides utility functions and Buffers about web streams API

00:05:32.487 --> 00:05:36.266
And http makes http servers easier to use

00:05:36.267 --> 00:05:42.009
Also there are functions regarding http errors, status codes and cookies

00:05:44.636 --> 00:05:49.849
Node.js has built-in modules that are similar to Deno's default library

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:55.053
These built-in modules have no versionings and is connected to Node.js's runtime

00:05:55.054 --> 00:05:57.797
So new versions can't be easily uploaded

00:05:57.798 --> 00:06:01.152
For example, when you want to upgrade your Node11

00:06:01.153 --> 00:06:04.691
to Node 14 or 16 in a project

00:06:04.692 --> 00:06:09.549
In most cases, when we update version, the code is not working

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:12.715
Node.js version can't be easily changed

00:06:12.716 --> 00:06:16.894
According to the runtime version the built-in module is fixed

00:06:16.895 --> 00:06:20.921
so it will be hard to use new functions

00:06:23.434 --> 00:06:27.141
Deno's standard library has versioning separate from runtime

00:06:27.142 --> 00:06:34.301
which allows for flexible versioning of the standard library while the runtime version is fixed

00:06:34.302 --> 00:06:39.283
Because it is released and imported like other third-party libraries

00:06:39.284 --> 00:06:44.183
as you can see by just uploading the library version

00:06:44.184 --> 00:06:47.380
you can update easily

00:06:50.955 --> 00:06:54.586
Next, Deno provides  Promise based asynchronous API

00:06:54.994 --> 00:07:00.163
The many asynchronous APIs provided by Node.js were made before promise

00:07:00.165 --> 00:07:03.342
so the main logic still uses the callback method

00:07:03.343 --> 00:07:07.495
So to use them in the Promise format promisify function in the util built-module

00:07:07.496 --> 00:07:10.671
has to be used

00:07:10.672 --> 00:07:14.259
so that the callback method API is transformed into the Promise format

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:17.138
or reprocessed APIs that returns the promise

00:07:17.139 --> 00:07:21.278
like fs/promises have to be used

00:07:21.776 --> 00:07:26.594
On Deno all asynchronous APIs are Promise based

00:07:26.595 --> 00:07:29.790
Therefore the callback style API is not provided at all

00:07:33.015 --> 00:07:37.614
Here readFile functions from Node.js and Deno are presented

00:07:37.615 --> 00:07:40.865
The Node.js will get callback

00:07:40.866 --> 00:07:45.330
get callback and make a second context that is readFile context

00:07:45.331 --> 00:07:51.084
and on the last line, the inner binding callback context will be rolled over

00:07:51.085 --> 00:07:53.353
which is how the callback is used

00:07:53.354 --> 00:07:55.439
On the other hand, Deno on the right,

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:58.993
from the beginning, has readFile function composed of async function

00:07:58.994 --> 00:08:03.610
and by using await and calling the inner asynchronous API

00:08:03.611 --> 00:08:08.760
it uses Promise based logic

00:08:10.412 --> 00:08:15.990
By looking at what I just showed you can tell that Deno's code is much simpler

00:08:15.991 --> 00:08:19.358
In Node.js, to change the readFile function in fs

00:08:19.359 --> 00:08:24.633
to a Promise format more codes has been added in Node.js

00:08:25.935 --> 00:08:27.956
All these codes are for changing into Promise

00:08:27.957 --> 00:08:33.207
codes used for changing this readFile to promises in fs promise

00:08:33.207 --> 00:08:37.916
So Deno is done with just the function on the right

00:08:39.477 --> 00:08:42.587
for returning the API to the promise format

00:08:42.589 --> 00:08:45.571
but Node has many codes like this

00:08:46.381 --> 00:08:51.009
Also, if there is an unfortunate situation and you have to look into the real code

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:54.821
maybe because there is a bug and you have to look through the inner code

00:08:54.822 --> 00:08:58.754
Deno's inner code will be much more convenient to look through

00:09:01.128 --> 00:09:06.750
Deno's standard library has a cleaner inner code

00:09:06.751 --> 00:09:09.208
As explained earlier, because Deno's standard library

00:09:09.208 --> 00:09:13.629
has no big difference between other third part libraries as providing methods,

00:09:13.630 --> 00:09:19.136
though it already should be like this, the functions in the standard library in Deno

00:09:19.137 --> 00:09:25.421
that would be mostly used will be helpful for debugging or understanding inner actions due to its simplicity

00:09:29.251 --> 00:09:32.704
Next, Deno strives to abide by the web platform API

00:09:33.085 --> 00:09:39.707
Web platform APIs are those built in web browsers such as Chrome or Firefox

00:09:40.385 --> 00:09:44.058
When running functions on runtime outside of web browsers,

00:09:44.058 --> 00:09:46.681
the same functions are often already included

00:09:47.253 --> 00:09:50.373
In this case, Deno does not recreate the wheel

00:09:50.374 --> 00:09:54.014
It implements the Web API that is compatible with the browser

00:09:54.015 --> 00:09:56.146
And it is co-compatible with the browser

00:09:57.593 --> 00:10:01.840
Like Fetch, Console, Streams, Web Storage, Web Worker

00:10:01.841 --> 00:10:04.351
Such Web API is already implemented in Deno

00:10:05.068 --> 00:10:09.543
Of course, at Node.js such Web API is partially implemented

00:10:09.544 --> 00:10:12.830
But most functions are still experimental, it’s either only partially compatible

00:10:12.831 --> 00:10:14.481
Or requires a third-party library

00:10:14.955 --> 00:10:19.339
And even if it is implemented, it exists as an internal module

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:21.818
Such internal modules should be called in for the use

00:10:23.573 --> 00:10:28.482
At Deno, the API used at browsers can be used right away

00:10:28.483 --> 00:10:31.580
So those who usually write browser API code

00:10:31.581 --> 00:10:36.243
Can easily write the code that runs at the runtime outside the browser on Deno

00:10:36.941 --> 00:10:41.471
Like this below, searching for Web API like localStorage at MDN

00:10:41.509 --> 00:10:46.139
Gives us the compatibility table at the bottom and we see Deno

00:10:50.443 --> 00:10:53.562
Next, the NPM package we use

00:10:53.563 --> 00:10:57.162
It can be a malicious code with various issues

00:10:57.163 --> 00:10:59.985
So in this case, at Node.js, this malicious code

00:10:59.986 --> 00:11:03.613
Can manipulate the system and the structure cannot respond to it properly

00:11:04.723 --> 00:11:08.857
At Deno, when file system, network, environment variable approach is needed

00:11:09.623 --> 00:11:14.678
And there is no appropriate  authority, it can stop the run, and avoid this issue

00:11:16.113 --> 00:11:18.451
And one of my favorite things

00:11:18.452 --> 00:11:21.495
It only supports ES Modules with the module system

00:11:22.518 --> 00:11:28.131
At module system, CommonJS, AMD, ES Modules, these are such module systems

00:11:28.132 --> 00:11:33.270
And these are, in my opinion, a big barrier into JavaScript for beginners

00:11:33.271 --> 00:11:35.989
At Deno, it only supports ESM

00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:40.411
So it is possible to write codes regardless of the previous module system

00:11:41.162 --> 00:11:46.213
In addition, as I said before, something with so many functions,

00:11:46.214 --> 00:11:51.249
The standard library is also written in ESModule, so regardless of the proficiency in JavaScript

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:53.929
It is easy to read the internal code

00:11:57.796 --> 00:12:00.620
I have talked about what strengths are there with Deno,

00:12:00.621 --> 00:12:03.501
And why it is good compared to Node.js

00:12:04.036 --> 00:12:06.191
However good it is,

00:12:06.192 --> 00:12:09.584
It’ll be even better to be able to use the broad ecosystem of Node.js, right?

00:12:10.093 --> 00:12:13.070
So in this section, I will introduce to you the method

00:12:13.071 --> 00:12:16.092
That allows each other’s code to be run in the opposite runtime

00:12:19.953 --> 00:12:23.689
There are many ways to run Node.js code at Deno

00:12:24.213 --> 00:12:28.612
First, let’s start with how to run the small code fragment

00:12:28.613 --> 00:12:31.905
Previously, the small code fragments run at Node.js

00:12:32.433 --> 00:12:34.877
Can be replaced with within the Deno standard library

00:12:34.878 --> 00:12:39.212
With node compatible modules for compatibility

00:12:39.213 --> 00:12:41.162
Not all functions are supported yet

00:12:41.163 --> 00:12:45.422
But as seen at the right, most modules are compatible

00:12:45.423 --> 00:12:51.542
And the compatibility is dealt with around popular libraries

00:12:51.543 --> 00:12:53.513
To increase the compatibility

00:12:57.703 --> 00:12:59.258
Not only the small code fragments

00:12:59.259 --> 00:13:02.312
The whole code written for Node.js can be run easily as well

00:13:03.123 --> 00:13:05.792
Deno has Node compatibility mode

00:13:05.793 --> 00:13:09.474
So it allows the use of CommonJS with the diverse global objects

00:13:10.253 --> 00:13:13.973
And Node.js API is, as I said,

00:13:13.974 --> 00:13:17.934
Not needed to change into the node compatibility module in the Deno’s standard library

00:13:17.935 --> 00:13:19.588
It automatically fills polyfill

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:25.002
To use compatibility mode, using deno run compact flag

00:13:25.003 --> 00:13:29.874
It can change the overall running condition into compatibility mode

00:13:31.303 --> 00:13:35.505
Like this, CommonJS code makes wrapper around it like below

00:13:35.506 --> 00:13:38.543
It assesses the whole code internally

00:13:39.069 --> 00:13:41.555
The example below has many things omitted

00:13:41.556 --> 00:13:45.825
But like the filename at the left, the special variable of CommonJS

00:13:45.826 --> 00:13:50.679
Or the process, the overall module, when it runs the right code

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:56.908
This Node CommonJS and its special things are all filled in

00:14:00.223 --> 00:14:02.630
Sometimes the big NPM Modules

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:05.695
You may want to import them from Deno code for use

00:14:05.993 --> 00:14:09.488
For that case, using a Deno-friendly CDN

00:14:09.489 --> 00:14:12.991
You need no other work to use Node.js module

00:14:13.795 --> 00:14:18.146
Deno-friendly CDN, for instance, is esm.sh or skypack

00:14:18.147 --> 00:14:22.892
When receiving module from this CDN, things done by Node compatibility mode

00:14:22.893 --> 00:14:25.875
Is now usable with the automatically processed modules

00:14:26.732 --> 00:14:32.167
This CDN automatically changes Common.JS-based codes into ESM

00:14:32.473 --> 00:14:34.713
And it provides typing for TypeScript

00:14:35.053 --> 00:14:36.113
Replace Various Node.js built-in module to std/node polyfill,
and then inject.

00:14:36.117 --> 00:14:40.570
Replace Various Node.js built-in module to std/node polyfill,
and then inject.

00:14:41.262 --> 00:14:44.321
Like you see at the very bottom,
if you import,

00:14:44.322 --> 00:14:48.625
You can get the NPM module that is automatically
changed to be usable in Deno.

00:14:52.181 --> 00:14:56.746
Previously, I introduced the
method of achieving compatibility with NPM module

00:14:56.747 --> 00:15:00.661
by using Node compatibility mode and Deno friendly CDN.

00:15:00.662 --> 00:15:04.570
Actually, from the most recent Deno 1.25.2,

00:15:04.571 --> 00:15:06.849
Node compatibility mode is removed,

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:12.747
and it was changed to a new method
of importing NPM module, called NPM specifier.

00:15:13.601 --> 00:15:18.042
If you use the new NPM specifier, unlike the Node compat mode,

00:15:18.043 --> 00:15:21.811
you no longer need to use special flag to change the overall settings,

00:15:21.812 --> 00:15:26.671
and you can use NPM module without relying on Deno-friendly CDN.

00:15:27.572 --> 00:15:30.553
The method of using is simpler compared to before.

00:15:31.032 --> 00:15:33.785
Like Deno friendly CDN had to put address such as esm.sh,

00:15:33.786 --> 00:15:37.091
in front of import route,

00:15:37.092 --> 00:15:41.460
You only need to put npm:, the NPM prefix.

00:15:42.042 --> 00:15:46.481
Like here at the bottom, put Deno run, then the "cowsay",

00:15:46.482 --> 00:15:51.052
which is a library where
a strange cow appears and says things.

00:15:51.053 --> 00:15:55.640
You can see the NPM module run by Deno run right away.

00:15:59.442 --> 00:16:04.126
The compatibility I have talked about so far,
was related to compatibility at the coding level.

00:16:05.052 --> 00:16:09.454
In the Node.js ecosystem, there are codes that use API of Node.js,

00:16:09.455 --> 00:16:11.615
but Node.js Addon exists, too.

00:16:12.621 --> 00:16:14.821
Addons that are written in low level language,

00:16:14.822 --> 00:16:19.913
since it uses Node API provided by node to manipulate JavaScript value,

00:16:19.914 --> 00:16:22.133
it should provide Node API compatibility.

00:16:22.134 --> 00:16:25.247
So that Addon could be used in other runtimes.

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:30.726
The PR for using of Node API compatibility is happening right now,

00:16:30.727 --> 00:16:36.036
if this PR is applied, among the many sqlite in NPM,

00:16:36.037 --> 00:16:38.198
sqlite using Addon,

00:16:38.199 --> 00:16:41.391
or image-processing libraries such as sharp,

00:16:41.392 --> 00:16:43.441
could be used on Deno.

00:16:47.202 --> 00:16:52.491
So far, it was how to use Node.js code in Deno.

00:16:52.492 --> 00:16:55.416
Now, we will look at methods of using
libraries that are written in Deno

00:16:55.417 --> 00:16:57.928
used in Node.js.

00:16:58.502 --> 00:17:03.401
There is Deno to node transform library called dnt.

00:17:03.402 --> 00:17:06.721
If you write a simple transform code using dnt,

00:17:06.722 --> 00:17:11.740
Deno code can be easily transformed to Node.js code.

00:17:14.952 --> 00:17:19.351
dnt rewrites import identifier of the module,

00:17:19.352 --> 00:17:22.153
and injects shim so that many resources in Deno namespace

00:17:22.154 --> 00:17:25.131
can be run in Node.js.

00:17:25.862 --> 00:17:29.231
The code on the left is the transform code
written by dnt,

00:17:29.712 --> 00:17:35.461
If you look from the top,
directory called npm is made,

00:17:37.742 --> 00:17:41.668
then build script is written consisting of
the package targeting this directory.

00:17:42.382 --> 00:17:45.306
The entry script in this build script
becoming the standard,

00:17:45.307 --> 00:17:49.388
Resolve and change the modules one by one to node.js code.

00:17:49.389 --> 00:17:53.248
Like you see at the bottom,
you can fill in contents that will be in package.json.

00:17:53.977 --> 00:17:58.132
This is not in the example, you can assign a specific npm module

00:17:58.133 --> 00:18:00.569
to a specific deno reliability

00:18:00.892 --> 00:18:04.646
If you have already written
node code in the original code,

00:18:04.647 --> 00:18:07.157
instead of the deno code you're currently using,

00:18:07.158 --> 00:18:09.350
you can enable node code.

00:18:12.832 --> 00:18:15.309
If there is no reliability on Deno API,

00:18:15.310 --> 00:18:18.837
which means Deno standard library
and external library is used,

00:18:18.838 --> 00:18:22.873
but those libraries are not directly using Deno API,

00:18:22.874 --> 00:18:24.847
dnt does not necessarily need to be used.

00:18:25.396 --> 00:18:28.984
Using plugged in called esbuild and esbuild_deno_loader,

00:18:28.985 --> 00:18:32.645
Deno code can be easily bundled with esbuild.

00:18:35.272 --> 00:18:37.842
Since esbuild has special Deno Wrapper,

00:18:37.843 --> 00:18:42.934
bundling code using esbuild on Deno script

00:18:42.935 --> 00:18:47.420
can be easily written like
using dnt to write transform code.

00:18:48.212 --> 00:18:51.186
The example at the bottom is,
among the Deno standard libraries,

00:18:51.187 --> 00:18:56.542
bundling SHA1 code using esbuild.

00:19:00.062 --> 00:19:03.969
Lastly, using Node compatibility like this,

00:19:03.970 --> 00:19:08.058
I will share an experience where I made development tooling using Deno

00:19:08.059 --> 00:19:10.958
that can be run on Node.js ecosystem.

00:19:11.310 --> 00:19:14.358
Our team made a development tooling called the Pbkit.

00:19:14.359 --> 00:19:18.076
Pbkit is a compilation of development tooling
related to Protocol buffers.

00:19:19.042 --> 00:19:23.705
Even though Pbkit requires various runtime
such as Node.js or browser,

00:19:23.706 --> 00:19:28.109
we could make Node.js tooling
without losing developer experience using Deno.

00:19:29.851 --> 00:19:33.510
All the codes of Pbkit's main repository are written in Deno

00:19:33.511 --> 00:19:37.250
There are 3 categories where we have to consider whether it's compatible with Node.js

00:19:37.761 --> 00:19:42.831
Pbkit core, pbkit codegen and Protobuf's language server

00:19:43.427 --> 00:19:45.686
All of them have different conditions

00:19:45.687 --> 00:19:48.485
so the code conversion should be done in different ways

00:19:48.731 --> 00:19:50.430
Let me introduce them one by one

00:19:53.181 --> 00:19:56.236
Pbkit core is not dependent on NPM

00:19:56.237 --> 00:19:59.326
and it was purposefully written in a way that it wouldn't be dependent on Deno

00:19:59.647 --> 00:20:04.344
Because the codes must be executed on the browser

00:20:04.345 --> 00:20:08.619
but if it's dependent on a particular vendor, then conversion can be a little hard

00:20:09.272 --> 00:20:11.432
So this Pbkit which isn't dependent on anything

00:20:11.433 --> 00:20:14.600
can be executed on a browser

00:20:14.601 --> 00:20:17.216
simply by removing the import request extensions

00:20:17.736 --> 00:20:21.169
Of course the conversion from TypeScript to JavaScript is

00:20:21.170 --> 00:20:25.400
should be done on the workflow of a client, a developer, or a user

00:20:25.401 --> 00:20:27.384
who uses the core codes

00:20:30.611 --> 00:20:34.515
And for Codegen, it must be bundled into a file

00:20:34.516 --> 00:20:38.626
and in this case, by using esbuild and esbuild_deno_loader,

00:20:38.627 --> 00:20:42.823
Deno codes can be bundled without any dependency,  resolution issues

00:20:42.824 --> 00:20:47.346
And in this case as well, there's no dependence to Deno API issues, so it's possible

00:20:51.311 --> 00:20:55.020
Next, Protocol buffers language server is what we made

00:20:55.021 --> 00:21:00.002
and it was made with the npm package that's called  vscode-jsonrpc

00:21:00.003 --> 00:21:04.604
but it can only be executed on vscode, which is Node.js

00:21:05.793 --> 00:21:07.454
so it was quite hard

00:21:07.455 --> 00:21:12.742
For this complex problem, LPS, which is Deno-dependent,

00:21:12.743 --> 00:21:17.347
I mean not LPS, but Deno-dependent Protobuf Language Server code,

00:21:17.348 --> 00:21:20.502
should be made into a single NPM module using dnt

00:21:20.503 --> 00:21:26.900
so vscode plugin changes to npm install pbkit LSP

00:21:26.901 --> 00:21:30.749
and it can be installed

00:21:32.291 --> 00:21:36.493
And also for projects that must be compatible with Node.js in different settings,

00:21:36.496 --> 00:21:38.740
we could use Deno without any issues

00:21:42.591 --> 00:21:45.423
So for the last step, can we use it for production?

00:21:46.071 --> 00:21:49.630
So when our team was developing the developing tools using Deno,

00:21:49.631 --> 00:21:54.813
we did not necessarily need NPM packages, so we could easily introduce Deno

00:21:54.814 --> 00:21:58.904
so if you develop a part that's not so dependent on the legacy ecosystem,

00:21:58.905 --> 00:22:02.031
then it wouldn't be a problem when introducing Deno to production

00:22:03.499 --> 00:22:06.457
If it's reliant on a particular NPM module,

00:22:06.458 --> 00:22:10.529
then please check whether it works

00:22:10.530 --> 00:22:12.820
when all the modules can be used by using all the methods introduced

00:22:13.741 --> 00:22:15.573
And if compatibility issues cannot be solved

00:22:15.574 --> 00:22:19.168
if you contribute to this problem, it'd be so much better

00:22:19.169 --> 00:22:23.769
but please list it up as the related issues for node compatibility on Deno

00:22:23.770 --> 00:22:27.311
and maintainers will help you solve the problem

00:22:29.220 --> 00:22:33.860
So there are some things to consider when you use the Node.js ecosystem

00:22:33.861 --> 00:22:37.251
but in contrast, it's not so hard to

00:22:37.252 --> 00:22:40.301
write codes that work on Node.js on Deno

00:22:40.661 --> 00:22:43.279
So we had to write those codes

00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:47.718
and just like pkbit, we had to make some strategies like separating dependencies and so on

00:22:48.127 --> 00:22:53.381
or using other codes such as dnt or esbuild depending on some situations

00:22:55.101 --> 00:23:00.200
So personally, I can use it without any complex settings

00:23:00.201 --> 00:23:04.820
but also I don't have to consider other legacy methods in the ecosystem

00:23:04.821 --> 00:23:09.120
And I got so much help from the standard library, too, while developing with Deno,

00:23:09.121 --> 00:23:14.136
so it was very fun writing JavaScript codes

00:23:14.711 --> 00:23:16.598
It doesn't have to be production

00:23:16.599 --> 00:23:20.193
You can start with little CLI tooling or blogs

00:23:20.535 --> 00:23:24.180
Try writing codes with Deno in any form

00:23:24.181 --> 00:23:27.335
and feel how amazing it is

00:23:28.401 --> 00:23:29.331
Thank you

